AN EMPTY LIST ITEM IS DROPPED, AND THE AUTHOR'S BULLET IS GONE.

WHAT md DOES TODAY. Three bullets in, two out:

    - One
    - Three

WHAT IT SHOULD DO. Keep the bullet. An item with no text is still an item the
author wrote, and `-` on its own line is how CommonMark spells one. Expected
here writes it bare rather than as `- `, so the line carries no trailing
whitespace.

WHY IT IS A MEANING DEFECT. A list of three becomes a list of two. On a
checklist that is an errand nobody will do, because the line that held it is
no longer in the file. `md format` writes the file, so the loss is permanent
after one run.

CAUSE. `MarkdownParser.collectListItems`, in `flushGatheredText`:

    guard !text.isEmpty || task != nil else { return }

An empty piece is dropped unless it carries a checkbox. That guard is right
about the pieces a nested list splits off — an item whose text runs out before
the nested list has nothing to emit — but it cannot tell those apart from an
item the author wrote empty. `md` already makes the exception for a checkbox,
because `format-keeps-an-item-that-is-only-a-checkbox` demanded it. An empty
item needs the same exception and has no flag to ask for it.

The fix has to distinguish "this item produced no piece here" from "this item
is empty". The item node is in hand at the point of the guard, so its child
count answers that question directly.

NOT the same defect as the continuation one, and not caused by its fix. This
guard predates `ListItem.continuation`; the flag only decides whether a piece
that DOES emit carries a marker.

See also `format-does-not-leave-a-blank-line-where-a-list-was`, which is what
the dropped item leaves behind.
